1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,950
So how does CoolSpend decide where each tree goes?

2
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:08,837
It starts with a demand field — a map of the ground that actually needs cooling.

3
00:00:08,837 --> 00:00:18,950
A cell counts only if it is hot, paved, and unshaded: felt temperature above twenty-six degrees, an impervious surface, and not already under a canopy.

4
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:25,675
Each cell's weight is simply its temperature minus twenty-six, so the hottest ground asks loudest for shade.

5
00:00:25,675 --> 00:00:28,937
Then comes the key idea: shade-gain.

6
00:00:28,937 --> 00:00:35,837
A tree earns credit only for the ground its real shadow actually covers, traced across many July sun angles.

7
00:00:35,837 --> 00:00:43,262
Because the sun sits to the south, shade falls north — so the engine plants trees south of the hotspot, not on top of it.

8
00:00:43,262 --> 00:00:44,937
Now the greedy.

9
00:00:44,937 --> 00:00:50,287
At every step it picks the single tree giving the most shade-gain per euro, then repeats.

10
00:00:50,287 --> 00:00:56,887
Shaded ground can't be cooled twice, so each new tree helps a little less — diminishing returns.

11
00:00:56,887 --> 00:01:06,737
That property, submodularity, carries a mathematical guarantee: this simple step-by-step rule reaches at least sixty-three percent of the perfect answer.

12
00:01:06,737 --> 00:01:13,375
It stops only when no affordable tree adds value, or the budget runs dry — never on a fixed counter.

